// Judging a scaling claim, and the entry point.
//
// A claim compares the smallest and the largest case carrying it -- the first
// of the smallest and the last of the largest, which is where idc.py's stable
// sort puts them -- and holds when the larger count is within the bound's
// growth of the smaller, times 4 for the fixed cost a small case is dominated
// by. idc.py's arithmetic, in its order, so the two agree on the boundary.
//
// Claims are judged only after every case has passed: a count from a case that
// failed measures nothing.
//
// The entry point runs every case, except where $IDTC_SKIP -- exactly one
// character per case -- has a '1': a case bin/idc has already seen pass under
// its key (mid/form/unique/dump/digest/keys/). A case carrying a claim never
// has a key, so its count is always measured. With $IDTC_PASSED the index of
// each case that passed is written there, one per line, which is the only
// thing bin/idc records a pass from. With $IDTC_TICK, a file descriptor number,
// one '.' is written to it after each case that ran or was skipped as not run
// -- the live counter bin/idc draws on a terminal; unset, nothing is written.
//
// Cases run callees first: by the height of their function's component in
// the call graph's DAG (0 for a component that calls no other, else one more
// than its tallest callee), then by index, so an SCC's cases and
// those of unrelated functions at one height keep their index order. The
// index k is still the case's identity everywhere -- IDTC_SKIP, IDTC_PASSED,
// the key lines -- only the order they are visited in changes. Once a case
// fails, a case whose function reaches that one's component through a call
// is not run: a failure below it already says what is wrong, and running it
// would only repeat that failure one frame up. It is counted as `not run` in the
// one summary line, which names the first failure ($IDC_SHOW_NOT_RUN lists each
// case instead of only counting them), never written to IDTC_PASSED, and fails
// the build like a failure does. A
// failure inside the case's own component skips nothing.

emit_tc_rest() {
  emit_cases();
  emit_tc_setup();
  print("static int idtc_claims(void) {\n    int bad = 0;");
} chain () {
  claim_body();
  print("    return bad;\n}");
  emit_tc_dirs();
} chain () {
  emit_tc_graph();
} return void;
given env_init {
  ():() then calls emit_tc_setup:() then calls claim_body:() then calls emit_tc_graph:()
  ():() then (import chkfail)[0]:(0)
}

// idtc_graph, which the entry point calls first: each case's component and
// the edges between components (mid/form/unique/dump/digest/graph/hash/dag/),
// from which idtc_plan derives the run order and idtc_culprit the skips.
// With no case there is no digest to read -- emit_tests builds it only for a
// harness that has one -- and no edge is needed.
emit_tc_graph() {
  int[] tgc = dg_case_comps();
  int[] tge = [];
  int tgn = 0;
} chain () {
  if(len(tgc) > 0) {
    tge = dg_comp_edges();
    tgn = (import dg_ctr)[1];
  }
  print("static void idtc_graph(void) {\n    static const int cc[] = {");
  int tgi = 0;
} chain () {
  while(tgi < len(tgc)) {
    print("    " + tgc[tgi] + ",");
    tgi = tgi + 1;
  }
  print("    -1};\n    static const int ce[] = {");
  tgi = 0;
} chain () {
  while(tgi < len(tge)) {
    print("    " + tge[tgi] + ", " + tge[tgi + 1] + ",");
    tgi = tgi + 2;
  }
  print("    -1};\n    idtc_cc = cc;\n    idtc_ce = ce;\n    idtc_nc = " + tgn + ";\n}");
} return void;
given dg_t_built ():() then prints:("static void idtc_graph(void) {\n    static const int cc[] = {\n    0,\n    0,\n    1,\n    1,\n    -1};\n    static const int ce[] = {\n    1, 0,\n    1, 0,\n    -1};\n    idtc_cc = cc;\n    idtc_ce = ce;\n    idtc_nc = 3;\n}\n")
given dg_t_rows ():() then prints:("static void idtc_graph(void) {\n    static const int cc[] = {\n    -1,\n    -1,\n    -1,\n    -1,\n    -1};\n    static const int ce[] = {\n    -1};\n    idtc_cc = cc;\n    idtc_ce = ce;\n    idtc_nc = 0;\n}\n")
